WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
This is the Technical Difficulties, we're

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:05.660
playing Citation Needed. Joining me in the studio today, he reads books, you know it's Chris Joel!

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.700
Hello!

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.240
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan, Gary Brannan!

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.980
Good evening...!

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.020
And the bounciest man on the internet, Matt Gray!

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.980
Hello YouTube...

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.000
In front of me, I have an article from Wikipedia, and these folks can't see it. All...

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.060
Matt!

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.080
Every fact they get right is a point and a ding [DING].

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.380
And theres a special prize for particularly good answers which is...

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.840
So undignified.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.400
Today's article is Thomas Trueblood.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.000
That's a fine Victorian name, I'm thinking...

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.060
Uh, you're right. Yes...

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.460
Born...I'll give you a point for that.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:36.960
Thank you! [DING]

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.400
Born 1856.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.780
Was he an engineer?

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.180
No...not at all, quite the opposite, in fact.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.800
A demolitions expert!

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.000
Okay, not literally the opposite.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.980
Was he going around mills, letting the steam out?

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.580
Fly free, water vapour! Begone from your...

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.800
Thomas Trueblood. So...Victorian not-an-engineer...

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.860
Yeah...he's very much in the liberal arts.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.000
Author.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.600
Uh, no. Not really. More to do with, um, spoken word than the written word.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.000
Was he president of the British Shouting Association?

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.500
Oh...you're not close enough for a point...but you're in the right area.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.200
American Oratorial Society.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.420
I will give you the point there. [DING] He's a Professor of Oratory and Elocution.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.200
And somehow I said that really, really, eloquently, because...

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.900
-- Oratory and elocution.
-- Elocution, darling(!)

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:24.940
Yes...

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.300
Um, Trueblood gave dramatic readings around the world.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.460
He did a performance of Hamlet.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.940
What might have been special about that?

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.980
All he did was rent out some pork products

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.460
Ham-let. Jesus(!)

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.560
I want it back, sithee...

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.200
Oh sorry, elocution. I would like that back when you're finished with it.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.020
First radio performance of Hamlet?

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.860
Little bit early for that, sadly. We're...1908.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.960
First slapstick performance of Hamlet?

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.700
Chuckle Brothers' Hamlet.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.960
To me, to you...

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.500
Do we have to explain the Chuckle Brothers to the rest of the internet?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.760
-- No.
-- They can Google it...it's fine.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.020
Don't Google it!

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:05.760
Yeah, do!

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.640
Don't!

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.380
It was simply a one-man, abridged production.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.400
Was it just really short?

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.080
I quote here, "It is his plan to keep the principal scenes of the play, narrating the unimportant parts."

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:16.960
Like it!

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.880
He literally just skipped the boring bits.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.620
"Hamlet, the highlights."

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:21.200
Yup!

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.900
I could do that...I could do that kind of Shakespeare!

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.900
"Everybody dies."

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.060
"Hamlet."

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:27.800
"Everybody dies..."

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.000
"Romeo and Juliet."

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.620
Yeah.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.000
The most notable thing in this article is just titled...

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.160
"The Jam Handy incident."

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.280
Who is Jam Handy?

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.000
Well, that is literally the question I would like to ask you.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.260
Because it's a name!

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.000
I assume it's some precursor to the Hamburglar.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.100
I'm for your delicious conserves!

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.320
"You can't stop me, Trueblood. Why...I don't know why you run a conserve business...

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.240
"but you see where I'm driving at..."

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.200
"You see where we're going with this..."

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.000
Did he savagely put down Jam Handy in a debate?

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.360
Ooh, yes. Sure.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.020
Was this a scandal because Jam Handy was of the upper class and Trueblood was of the middle class?

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.220
Well, you wouldn't want to fight him...

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.720
'cause Jam Handy's damn handy!

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.200
It's a wonderful plot...

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.400
but no it's not.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.800
Jam Handy was a freshman student,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.100
17 years old.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.040
Henry Jamison Handy,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.200
hence Jam Handy.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.480
Yeah, if I had that kind of a name, I think I'd go with Jam Handy as well, so yeah.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.000
He got a bronze in the 1904 Olympics.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.020
Was that the Olympics where they had to stop the marathon, because the bloke drove a car and won it?

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.020
Um, give me a moment...

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:39.800
There was one of those Olympics where the marathon was called off

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.920
'cause some bloke blatantly ran the first hundred yards, got in a car, drove it

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.140
That's much more common in the Tour de France in the early years as well.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:46.640
Yeah.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.480
Gary...

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.320
No way!

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:50.660
Nice!

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:51.160
Really?

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:51.860
Smooth!

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.060
1904 Summer Olympics,  Frederick Lorz rode the rest of the way

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.080
in a car, to retrieve his clothes, after dropping out after nine miles.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.060
So he dropped out at nine miles, rode the rest of the way,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.360
then the car broke down at mile 19, and he just started jogging again!

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.360
To be fair, Lorz went on to win the Boston marathon, but the actual winner

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.060
was a Brit called Thomas Hicks

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.880
who was a Brit running for the US.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:17.900
But he had been...

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.120
doped, it would be in modern terms.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.320
He was supported by his trainers when he crossed the finish...

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.700
What had they given him?

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:25.420
Brandy!

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.240
Yes! [DING]

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.420
Spot on with brandy. Have the point. What had they mixed it with?

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:29.280
Beans!

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.000
Cocaine!

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.240
Ketamine!

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.060
Margarine!

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.000
Rat poison.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:35.940
Bollocks!

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.260
-- What?!
-- Harsh!

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.180
Strychnine sulfate.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:39.960
Right...

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.500
Poisoned you in just the right way, if done in just the right amount?

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.340
Yup!

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.540
He possibly would have died in the stadium, had he not been treated by several doctors.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.200
Why did you say brandy?

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:54.440
Because it just seems to be...
it's just the standard pick-me-up.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.040
Again, 'cause I do cycling and follow cycling...

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.400
Early days of the Tour de France...

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.520
Stop, have a brandy, crack back on!

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.600
It's traditional sort of mind, brain, and nerve tonic. You know...

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.040
It gets worse. 'Cause we had Frederick Lorz, rode some of the way in a car...

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:12.200
Thomas Hicks, technically won,
but doped by modern terms

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.020
Felix Carvajal -- I might've mispronounced that --
is a Cuban postman.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:21.960
Arrived at the last minute, ran in his street clothes. What kind of slowed him down?

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.660
His bag full of post.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:24.920
And his hat.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.160
Getting attacked by every dog on the way.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.000
Yeah.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:28.920
Yes.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.400
Getting it off all the ladies on the way.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.020
Oh hang on, yeah.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:31.640
Yeah.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.080
He just picked up a sarnie on the way?

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.560
Not quite a sarnie...

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.740
Tackled a cow!

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:36.580
Had a bite.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.240
No, not quite. It was a vegetable, rather than an animal

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.280
Potato?

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.780
He stood in a marrow?

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.000
Just clomped along with it on his foot!

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.620
"I've been attacked by a squash!"

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.000
"How am I meant to run in these marrowy clogs?!"

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.340
What do I need this for?

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.040
I mean the clogs are the main problem there, really.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.280
I mean, I know technically the Dutch marathon team do use them, but...

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:57.980
Can you imagine...

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.040
the best thing about the English language...

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.180
nowhere else in the world is anyone going to yell today...

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.800
"How do I win in these marrowy clogs?"

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.940
Marrowy Clogs actually came fifth.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.920
Right now, we need to smash cut to somewhere in the Netherlands.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.960
"How do I run in these marrowy clogs?!"

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.460
"No one else say that today..."

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.960
No, he stopped off to have some apples that he took from an orchard.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.360
Why did those actually slow him down a little bit?

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:29.480
Arsenic poisoning?

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.380
Uh...why arsenic?

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.520
There's...in apple seeds is that arsenic?

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.480
-- Cyanide.
-- Cyanide! Sorry, wrong thing, right idea.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.420
Were they a lot of crab apples?

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.540
Did they give him...like a dodgy tummy?

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.800
Um, they did give him a dodgy tummy.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:41.480
They were just rotten.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:42.380
Oh, you idiots.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:43.300
Well that wasn't very bright...

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.580
You would know if you picked up a rotten apple!

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.380
He had to lie down and take a nap!

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:50.620
And here's the thing...

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.160
this is why you say 1904...this is possibly why it stuck in your head,

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.260
because there were so many things...

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.420
despite that, he finished fourth!

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.260
How?!

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.660
So someone's had a nap, the other guy got in the car...

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.140
So presumably he actually finished second because the first two cheated...

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.960
Well, uh, no. The guy who...

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.680
Doping wasn't technically an offense at that point.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:09.180
Oh, okay.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.100
There wasn't synthetic drugs there, they'd given him an incredibly dangerous cocktail

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.820
and no one had thought to put that in the rules.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.140
Is this all the same race?

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.220
This is all the same race!

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.940
This is the 1904 Olympic marathon.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.540
Now you said, "Was he chased by dogs?"

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.460
The postman wasn't...

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.380
one of the other runners was!

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.600
Nearly a mile off course.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.860
Um, one of the first two black Africans to compete in the Olympics.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.780
Uh, Len Tau was chased a mile off course by aggressive dogs.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.180
You've got a dog Benny Hill,

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:42.600
you've got a man cheating in a car,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.220
you've got a bloke on brandy,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.260
another guy who's had a nap.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.820
And they weren't even part of the Olympics!

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.020
The Africans running the marathon, they just...

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:54.700
they were students from South Africa who'd been brought over as part of an exhibit on the Boer War

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.280
that was next to the Olympics!

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:57.160
Had they just thought "We'll have a go."

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.180
"We'll have a go!"

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.480
"It's only 26 miles."

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:04.060
Why...why has this not been serialized for television?!

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.440
Guys, I think we found the format...

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.240
Yes!

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.560
Um, a long time ago, folks, we were talking about Jam Handy.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:14.420
Oh, we were!

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.540
Oh, what was he about again?

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:17.680
Refresh my memory.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.020
He got a bronze in the 1904 Olympics in swimming.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:24.160
But what we're actually talking about is what he did to Thomas Trueblood.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.760
Did he heckle him in a particularly hilarious fashion?

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.480
Um, not in person.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.300
Started a scandal about him.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:30.980
Ooh, yeah, surely.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.460
Yes, have a point. [DING]

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.040
-- Yay!
-- Yes.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.680
"Once mispronounced something...
if you know what I mean."

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.960
I'm going to give you another point, because it really is [DING] "if you know what I mean".

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.300
Trueblood was teaching, and I'm going to quote here,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.420
"The delivery of short extracts from masterpieces of oratory".

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:49.780
Which was from a scene from a play,

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.880
in which a man kneels in front of a woman, pleading for her hand.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:54.220
Okay?

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.820
Nothing particularly off-colour about that.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.700
Unfortunately, Handy then wrote an article for a newspaper.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.500
Does anyone want to guess what he actually said was going on there?

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.580
Oh! Some kind of dirty filth?

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.620
That was not fit for the stage.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:08.780
Yeah, you can have a point there. [DING]

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:13.020
He said that students at Ann Arbor were taking lessons in lovemaking.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.200
A lesson in Victorian lovemaking would be: man is knelt in that fashion,

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:17.700
lights go off,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.400
someone goes ouch, lights come back on,

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.320
she's got a baby.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.960
That's basically how it works in those days, I think. That's accurate.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.220
It's how it works now, isn't it?

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:32.780
That article did the 1900s equivalent of going viral, because...

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.060
Sold three copies!

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.120
Yeah, well every...the local newspaper picked it up, then the national newspaper picked it up,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.700
and all of a sudden, Trueblood is sitting there,

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.340
in his superior's office
with all these cuttings around him going

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:46.940
"I didn't do this!"

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.560
I quote here, from the Chicago Tribune...

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.460
"Trueblood has nearly worn out his trousers at the knees."

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.080
You've got to pay your way through college somehow...

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:02.780
We've all been there, eh, fellows?

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.600
Um, what did Handy...

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.120
Um, Handy went on to do many things,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.920
um, he went on to be athletic.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.980
What was his main job?

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:14.760
Parachutist.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:15.560
Public Relations.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.620
Aww...

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.700
There's this lovely picture here of the last...

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.900
you know the vanity card, the last thing you see, when the logo comes up, and the...

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.400
Oh yeah. Okay. Yeah

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.160
"A Jam Handy picture."

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:30.800
At the end of the show, uh...

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.040
Oh! It's over! Thank God it's over!

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.720
Congratulations, Chris! You win this week.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:35.880
Yay!

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.080
You win a miniature library,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:38.320
to be inserted rectally

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.660
from which you can shoot the President of the United States.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.800
It's a "Texas Book Suppository".

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:49.220
I was with you... I was against you...
It's just about come back...

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:50.980
Thank you very much to Chris Joel.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:51.720
Thank you very much.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:52.780
To Gary Brannan.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.080
To Matt Gray.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:54.580
Buh bye!

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.760
That's been the Technical Difficulties,

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:56.400
I've been Tom Scott,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:57.360
and we'll see you next time!

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.700
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

